---
import { Code } from 'astro:components'

import { examples } from '../examples.ts'
import Gallery from '../layouts/Gallery.astro'
import Shell from '../layouts/Shell.astro'
import { componentLabel, mainMaxWidth } from '../siteMeta.ts'
// Measured, not asserted. `pnpm measure-chrome-bundle` bundles both chrome
// entry points for real and writes this file; CI re-checks it, so the figures
// below cannot drift from what the code costs. `~repo` is the repo root, see
// astro.config.mjs -- and because it is a typed JSON import, a renamed field
// fails `astro check` instead of rendering "undefined KB".
import bundle from '~repo/scripts/chromeBundleSizes.json'

// The engine call, spelled out. Every example page repeats this rather than
// importing it from somewhere in this site, so each page's source is complete
// and runs on its own.
const engineCode = `import { createViewState } from '@jbrowse/react-linear-genome-view2'

const state = createViewState({
  assembly: {
    name: 'volvox',
    uri: 'https://jbrowse.org/genomes/volvox/volvox.2bit',
  },
  tracks: [myTrackConfig],
})

// a LinearGenomeViewModel: knows bpPerPx, offsetPx, displayedRegions, and how
// to zoomTo/horizontalScroll. Draws nothing.
const { view } = state.session

// the same declarative path a URL launch or a saved session takes, so the
// assembly load, the navigation and the track show-ing happen in order
view.setInit({
  assembly: 'volvox',
  loc: 'ctgA:1..50,000',
  tracks: ['my_track_id'],
})
`
---

<Shell
  title="Build your own"
  componentLabel={componentLabel}
  examples={examples}
  mainMaxWidth={mainMaxWidth}
>
  <h1>Build your own genome browser</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    JBrowse ships a finished linear genome browser, and most people should use
    it. But underneath it is a set of parts, and if you are embedding genomic
    data in an app that already has its own design system, you may want the
    parts instead. These pages take the browser apart and put it back together
    one piece at a time.
  </p>

  <div class="doc">
    <h2>The two halves</h2>
    <p>
      A genome browser is an <strong>engine</strong> and some <strong
        >chrome</strong
      >. The engine resolves assemblies, chooses adapters, fetches and parses
      off the main thread, and keeps the coordinate state. The chrome is the
      search box, the ruler, the track labels, the loading spinner: everything
      around the data.
    </p>
    <p>
      The engine is the hard half and you should not rebuild it. One call gets
      you all of it, plus a view model that knows where it is in the genome and
      draws nothing:
    </p>
    <Code code={engineCode} lang="ts" />
    <p>
      From there every page in the sidebar adds exactly one thing. By the last
      one you have a browser with pan, zoom, three kinds of track, your own
      status overlays, your own ruler, your app driving it from outside, and
      clicks coming back out into a panel you wrote.
    </p>

    <h2>What you actually save</h2>
    <p>
      Bundling the chrome layer on its own, with React and MobX external, it
      costs <strong>{bundle.muiGzipKb} KB gzipped</strong> ({bundle.muiKb} KB raw)
      with JBrowse's own status components and <strong
        >{bundle.plainGzipKb} KB gzipped</strong
      > ({bundle.plainKb} KB raw) with plain ones. The difference is almost entirely
      Material UI. Both figures are the eager, pre-first-paint cost: no renderer and
      no display is in either, because <code>RenderingComponent</code> is lazy in
      every plugin.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those numbers are produced by <code>pnpm measure-chrome-bundle</code>,
      which builds both entry points and writes the result to a committed file
      this page reads. CI re-runs it, so the figures above cannot drift away
      from what the code costs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Bundle size is the smaller half of the argument. The larger half is that
      nothing on screen has to be talked out of looking like a Material widget:
      with both plain sets installed, JBrowse's own unforked wiggle, feature and
      alignments displays render <strong>no Material UI at all</strong>. That is
      checked rather than asserted — <code>pnpm smoke</code> in this site counts what
      each page renders, at rest and with the pointer over a track, and fails if the
      number moves.
    </p>

    <h2>What you do not get rid of</h2>
    <p>
      Two honest limits, both of which these pages hit and neither of which is
      hidden in them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The palette.</strong> Stock feature and alignments displays read a palette
      to colour their own <em>content</em> (reading frames, highlight boxes), so they
      need <code>resolvePalette</code> supplied through
      <code>PaletteProvider</code>. That is a set of colour strings, not a UI
      toolkit, and swapping the overlays changes the status UI rather than the
      colours. A wiggle track needs neither; the first two pages here supply no
      palette at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The engine.</strong> Nothing on this site rebuilds adapters, the RPC
      layer, or the fetch lifecycle, and you should not either. What is on offer is
      control of the chrome, not a smaller JBrowse.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Gallery examples={examples} />
</Shell>
